A CHECKBOX NEVER LANDS ON A PARAGRAPH THAT HAD NONE.

The block model is flat, so an item holding a nested list is split into the
piece before the list and the piece after it. The checkbox belongs to the
piece that holds the item's FIRST paragraph and to no other. Hand it to every
piece and the tail paragraph comes back as `- [x] Tail paragraph of the
parent.`: the document then asserts that a task is DONE which nobody ever
wrote as a task, let alone ticked.

That is worse than dropping a line. A reader who loses a line can see that
something is missing. A reader handed an invented finished task has no way to
know it is false.

The guard is the whole reason this case exists, and it holds today. Nothing
else in cli-tests pins it, so a later change to how items are split could put
the box back on the tail with the suite still green.

WHAT THE EXPECTED BYTES DO AND DO NOT CLAIM.

They pin the NARROW claim: the tail keeps its text and gains no checkbox.

They do NOT endorse the rest of the shape. The tail is written as a top-level
bullet of its own, and it was authored as a continuation paragraph INSIDE the
first item. That is a separate open defect, tracked by
`format-keeps-a-tail-paragraph-inside-its-item` and
`blocks-counts-an-item-split-by-a-nested-list-once`. When that defect is
fixed, the expected bytes here change, and the claim this case guards must
still hold: no checkbox on a paragraph that had none.
